# Early Medieval polity profiles, part 1 (Rome, Germanic kingdoms, Britain & Ireland)
P = []
def add(**k): P.append(k)

add(id="western-roman", name="Western Roman Empire", type="empire", yearsActive="395–476",
 summary="The Latin half of the Roman Empire after 395, ruled from Milan and then Ravenna, which lost its provinces to federate Germanic kingdoms until the last emperor in Italy was deposed in 476.",
 description="When Theodosius I died in 395 the empire was split between his sons, and the West went to the boy Honorius under the general Stilicho. Imperial government moved to Ravenna in 402, behind its marshes. Over the next eighty years Britain was abandoned (c. 410), Gaul and Spain were settled by Visigoths, Burgundians, Franks and Sueves, and Africa fell to the Vandals in 439, taking the grain and tax base with it. Real power passed to generals such as Aetius and Ricimer, and in 476 Odoacer deposed Romulus Augustulus and sent the regalia to Constantinople.",
 conflicts="Alaric's Visigoths sacked Rome in 410; the Rhine frontier collapsed after the crossing of 406. Aetius, with Visigothic and Frankish allies, halted Attila at the Catalaunian Plains in 451. The Vandals sacked Rome in 455, and two great expeditions against Vandal Africa (461 and 468) failed.",
 leaders="Honorius (395–423) ruled through Stilicho until Stilicho's execution in 408. Valentinian III (425–455) and his general Flavius Aetius held Gaul together for a generation. Ricimer made and unmade emperors (456–472); Romulus Augustulus (475–476) was the last emperor in Italy.",
 religion="Nicene Christianity was the state religion after Theodosius's edicts of 380–392. The bishops of Rome, notably Leo I (440–461), gained authority as civil government weakened, while most Germanic federates were Arian Christians.",
 culture="Latin was the language of law, church and administration. A senatorial aristocracy kept vast estates and villas, while towns shrank and coinage thinned. The Notitia Dignitatum lists the offices and army units of this late imperial state.",
 literature="Augustine of Hippo wrote the City of God (413–426) in answer to the sack of Rome. Claudian praised Stilicho in Latin verse, and Rutilius Namatianus described a journey through ravaged Italy in 417.",
 art="Ravenna's Mausoleum of Galla Placidia (c. 425–450) keeps some of the finest early Christian mosaics. Consular ivory diptychs and gold-glass medallions show the late Roman court style.",
 ideas="Augustine separated the earthly city from the city of God, arguing that Christian hope did not depend on Rome's survival. His writings on grace and the just war shaped Latin thought for a thousand years.",
 science="Late Roman encyclopedists such as Martianus Capella (On the Marriage of Philology and Mercury) arranged the liberal arts that medieval schools would teach. Vegetius summarized Roman military practice in his Epitome of Military Science.",
 story=[{"title":"The Regalia Go East","text":"In 476 Odoacer did not crown himself emperor. He sent the purple robes and diadem to Constantinople with a message that one emperor was enough, and ruled Italy as a king under the emperor Zeno's distant authority."}],
 images=[("Mausoleo di Galla Placidia, interno, mosaico del cielo stellato.jpg","Starry-sky mosaic, Mausoleum of Galla Placidia, Ravenna (c. 425–450)","art"),
         ("Romulus Augustus (Tremissis).jpg","Gold tremissis of Romulus Augustulus, the last western emperor in Italy","culture")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Ancient Rome: The fall of the Western Empire'; P. Heather, The Fall of the Roman Empire (2005).")

add(id="byzantium", name="Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Empire", type="empire", yearsActive="395–1453",
 nameByTick={"400":"Eastern Roman Empire","500":"Eastern Roman Empire","600":"Eastern Roman Empire"},
 summary="The Roman Empire in the east, ruled from Constantinople, which reconquered Italy and Africa under Justinian, then lost Syria, Egypt and North Africa to the Arabs and became the Greek-speaking Byzantine state.",
 description="Constantinople's walls, fleet and tax system let the eastern empire survive the fifth-century invasions that broke the West. Justinian I (527–565) codified Roman law, built Hagia Sophia and retook Africa from the Vandals and Italy from the Ostrogoths. Plague, a long Persian war and the Arab conquests of the 630s–640s cut the empire back to Anatolia, the Balkan coasts, Sicily and parts of Italy. Under Heraclius and his successors Greek replaced Latin in government, and the themes system put soldiers on the land. By 900 the Macedonian dynasty was beginning a revival.",
 conflicts="Wars with Sasanian Persia ended in Heraclius's victory at Nineveh (627). Arab fleets besieged Constantinople in 674–678 and 717–718 and were beaten back, partly with Greek fire. Bulgar khan Krum killed Emperor Nikephoros I in battle in 811.",
 leaders="Theodosius II (408–450) built the land walls and issued the Theodosian Code. Justinian I (527–565) and Theodora led the reconquests. Heraclius (610–641) defeated Persia but lost the east to the Arabs. Leo III (717–741) broke the second Arab siege; Basil I (867–886) founded the Macedonian dynasty.",
 religion="Orthodox Christianity under the patriarch of Constantinople. Councils at Ephesus (431) and Chalcedon (451) defined Christ's nature and split off the Miaphysite churches of Egypt and Syria. Iconoclasm (726–787 and 814–843) divided the church until icons were restored in 843.",
 culture="Greek became the language of state by the seventh century, though citizens still called themselves Romans. Constantinople was the largest city in Christian Europe, with its Hippodrome factions, silk workshops and guilds regulated in the Book of the Eparch.",
 literature="Procopius wrote the official Wars and the scandalous Secret History of Justinian's reign. Romanos the Melodist composed sung sermons (kontakia). Patriarch Photios (9th century) summarized hundreds of ancient books in his Bibliotheca.",
 art="Hagia Sophia (532–537) set the model of the domed church. The San Vitale mosaics in Ravenna show Justinian and Theodora with their courts. After Iconoclasm, icon painting and church mosaics returned in a stricter, more formal style.",
 ideas="Justinian's Corpus Juris Civilis (529–534) preserved Roman law and later became the basis of Western civil law. Theologians such as John of Damascus defended icons as windows on the holy, not idols.",
 science="Anthemius of Tralles and Isidore of Miletus, the architects of Hagia Sophia, were also mathematicians. Leo the Mathematician taught at the Magnaura school in the ninth century, and Greek fire, a petroleum weapon, was developed around 672.",
 story=[{"title":"Nika!","text":"In January 532 the Blue and Green chariot factions joined forces and burned half of Constantinople, shouting 'Nika!' (conquer). Justinian was ready to flee, but Theodora reportedly said that royalty makes a fine burial shroud. Belisarius trapped the rioters in the Hippodrome, and the rebuilt Hagia Sophia rose from the ruins."}],
 images=[("Hagia Sophia Mars 2013.jpg","Hagia Sophia, Constantinople, built 532–537","art"),
         ("Meister von San Vitale in Ravenna 003.jpg","Justinian I and his court, mosaic in San Vitale, Ravenna (c. 547)","culture")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Byzantine Empire'; J. Herrin, Byzantium (2007).")

add(id="visigoths", name="Visigothic Kingdom", type="kingdom", yearsActive="418–721",
 summary="A Gothic people who sacked Rome in 410, settled in Aquitaine as Roman federates and then ruled Hispania from Toledo until the Arab-Berber conquest of 711.",
 description="After defeating Rome at Adrianople (378) and sacking Rome under Alaric (410), the Visigoths were settled around Toulouse in 418. Clovis's Franks drove them out of most of Gaul at Vouillé (507), and the kingdom moved its centre to Toledo. Leovigild (568–586) conquered the Suevic kingdom in Galicia, and his son Reccared converted to Catholicism in 587. Church councils at Toledo and a single law code bound Goths and Hispano-Romans together, but disputed successions left the kingdom open to the invasion of 711.",
 conflicts="Vouillé (507) cost them Toulouse. Byzantine troops held the south-east coast from 552 until c. 625. Tariq ibn Ziyad defeated King Roderic at the Guadalete in 711.",
 leaders="Alaric I (395–410) sacked Rome; Euric (466–484) issued the first Gothic law code. Leovigild (568–586) united the peninsula; Reccared I (586–601) converted to Catholicism. Recceswinth (649–672) issued the Liber Iudiciorum; Roderic (710–711) was the last king.",
 religion="Arian Christianity, inherited from the missionary Ulfilas, until Reccared accepted the Nicene faith at the Third Council of Toledo (589). Later councils passed harsh laws against Jews.",
 culture="A Gothic elite ruled a much larger Hispano-Roman population. Gothic gave way to Latin, and intermarriage was legalized under Leovigild. Kings were elected by nobles and bishops and anointed at Toledo.",
 literature="Isidore of Seville wrote the Etymologies, an encyclopedia used across medieval Europe, and a History of the Goths. The Liber Iudiciorum (654) was a written law code for all subjects.",
 art="The votive crowns of the Treasure of Guarrazar, with gold, sapphires and pearls, hung in churches near Toledo. Small horseshoe-arched churches such as San Juan de Baños (661) survive.",
 ideas="Isidore described the king as bound by law and warned that a ruler who acts unjustly is no king. The Toledo councils made the church partner in choosing and anointing kings.",
 science="Isidore's Etymologies and On the Nature of Things passed on Roman knowledge of astronomy, medicine and geography, including the T-O world map.",
 story=[{"title":"Recceswinth's Crown","text":"In 1858 a farmer near Toledo found gold crowns in a field at Guarrazar. One spells out in hanging gold letters RECCESVINTHUS REX OFFERET: 'King Recceswinth offers this'. It had been hidden, probably as Arab armies approached in 711."}],
 images=[("Corona de Recesvinto (M.A.N.) 01.jpg","Votive crown of King Recceswinth, Treasure of Guarrazar (7th century)","art"),
         ("San Juan de Baños (Palencia) 01.jpg","San Juan de Baños, a Visigothic church dedicated in 661","culture")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Visigoth'; R. Collins, Visigothic Spain 409–711 (2004).")

add(id="ostrogoths", name="Ostrogothic Kingdom", type="kingdom", yearsActive="493–553",
 nameByTick={"700":"Crimean Goths"},
 summary="The Gothic kingdom of Theodoric the Great in Italy, which kept Roman government running at Ravenna before Justinian destroyed it in a twenty-year war. A Gothic remnant survived in Crimea.",
 description="The Ostrogoths had been subjects of the Huns before Theodoric led them to Italy with the emperor Zeno's blessing, killing Odoacer in 493. From Ravenna he ruled Goths under Gothic law and Romans under Roman officials, with the senator Cassiodorus as his secretary. After his death in 526, Justinian's generals Belisarius and Narses invaded in 535, and the war wrecked Italy until the last Gothic king Teia fell in 552. Gothic-speaking communities in Crimea lasted into early modern times.",
 conflicts="Theodoric's war against Odoacer (489–493). The Gothic War (535–554), with sieges of Rome and Naples, ended at Taginae (552) and Mons Lactarius (552–553).",
 leaders="Theodoric the Great (471–526); his daughter Amalasuntha, regent for her son (526–534), who was murdered; Witigis (536–540); Totila (541–552), who briefly retook most of Italy.",
 religion="Arian Christians alongside a Catholic Roman majority. Ravenna had separate Arian and Catholic cathedrals and baptisteries.",
 culture="Goths formed the army and Romans staffed the civil service. The Roman Senate continued to meet, and Theodoric repaired aqueducts and walls.",
 literature="Boethius wrote The Consolation of Philosophy (c. 524) in prison before Theodoric had him executed. Cassiodorus's Variae preserve the kingdom's official letters, and the Gothic Silver Bible (Codex Argenteus) was probably copied in Ravenna.",
 art="Sant'Apollinare Nuovo and the Arian Baptistery in Ravenna were Theodoric's foundations. His mausoleum is roofed with a single limestone block about 10 metres across.",
 ideas="Boethius translated and commented on Aristotle's logic and argued that true happiness lies in God, not fortune. Cassiodorus founded the Vivarium monastery to copy secular and sacred books.",
 science="Boethius's textbooks on arithmetic and music (De institutione arithmetica, De institutione musica) were standard in medieval schools.",
 story=[{"title":"The Silver Bible","text":"Bishop Ulfilas invented a Gothic alphabet in the fourth century to translate the Bible. A sixth-century copy written in silver and gold ink on purple parchment, the Codex Argenteus, travelled from Italy to Prague and is now in Uppsala."}],
 images=[("Mausoleum of Theodoric (Ravenna).jpg","Mausoleum of Theodoric, Ravenna (c. 520)","art"),
         ("Codex argenteus.jpg","Codex Argenteus, the Gothic Silver Bible (6th century)","culture")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Ostrogoth' and 'Theodoric'; P. Heather, The Goths (1996).")

add(id="franks", name="Frankish Kingdom", type="kingdom", yearsActive="481–843 (Middle Francia to 855)",
 nameByTick={"800":"Carolingian Empire","900":"Middle Francia (Lotharingia, Burgundy, Italy)"},
 summary="The kingdom founded by the Merovingian Clovis, which became the Carolingian Empire under Charlemagne and was divided among his grandsons in 843.",
 description="Clovis I (481–511) united the Frankish groups, defeated the last Roman ruler in Gaul, the Alamanni and the Visigoths, and was baptized a Catholic. Merovingian kings divided the realm into Neustria, Austrasia and Burgundy, while palace mayors gained real power. Charles Martel beat an Umayyad army near Poitiers (732), and his son Pippin became king in 751 with papal approval. Charlemagne conquered the Lombards, Saxons and Avars and was crowned emperor in Rome on Christmas Day 800. The Treaty of Verdun (843) split the empire into West, Middle and East Francia.",
 conflicts="Clovis's wars against Syagrius (486), the Alamanni and the Visigoths (507). Poitiers (732). Charlemagne's Saxon wars (772–804), including the massacre at Verden (782), and the destruction of the Avar khaganate (791–796). Vikings sacked Paris in 845.",
 leaders="Clovis I (481–511); Dagobert I (629–639); Charles Martel, mayor of the palace (718–741); Pippin the Short (751–768); Charlemagne (768–814); Louis the Pious (814–840); Lothar I, emperor and king of Middle Francia (840–855).",
 religion="Catholic since Clovis's baptism at Reims (c. 496–508). Irish and Anglo-Saxon missionaries such as Columbanus and Boniface founded monasteries. Charlemagne required tithes, preaching and baptism of conquered Saxons.",
 culture="Salic law set wergild payments for crimes. Counts administered districts, and missi dominici checked on them. Charlemagne's coin reform made the silver penny (denarius) the standard coin of western Europe.",
 literature="Gregory of Tours wrote the Ten Books of Histories (6th century). Einhard's Life of Charlemagne and the Royal Frankish Annals record the court. Carolingian minuscule, a clear new script, was used to copy most surviving classical Latin texts.",
 art="The Palatine Chapel at Aachen (consecrated 805) copied Byzantine models. Illuminated books such as the Godescalc Evangelistary and the Lorsch Gospels came from the court school.",
 ideas="Alcuin of York led the palace school and the Carolingian revival of learning. The Admonitio generalis (789) ordered schools at monasteries and cathedrals. The 800 coronation revived the idea of a western Roman emperor.",
 science="Monasteries kept computus, the calculation of Easter dates, and studied Bede's works on time. The Plan of St Gall (c. 820) shows an idealized monastery with infirmary, herb garden and workshops.",
 story=[{"title":"The Vase of Soissons","text":"Gregory of Tours tells how a soldier smashed a church vase that Clovis wanted to return to a bishop, saying the king had no right to more than his share of loot. A year later, at an inspection, Clovis split the man's head with an axe, saying: 'Thus you did to the vase at Soissons.'"}],
 images=[("Aachener Dom Oktogon.jpg","Octagon of Charlemagne's Palatine Chapel, Aachen (consecrated 805)","art"),
         ("Karolingische Minuskel.jpg","Carolingian minuscule script","culture")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Frank', 'Merovingian dynasty', 'Charlemagne'; R. McKitterick, Charlemagne (2008).")

add(id="burgundians", name="Kingdom of the Burgundians", type="kingdom", yearsActive="411–534",
 summary="An East Germanic people who settled first on the Rhine at Worms and then in the Rhône valley around Geneva and Lyon, where they ruled until the Franks conquered them in 534.",
 description="The Burgundians crossed the Rhine in 406 and set up a kingdom around Worms. In 436–437 Aetius used Hunnic troops to crush them, a disaster remembered in the Nibelungenlied. Aetius then resettled the survivors in Sapaudia (Savoy), from where they expanded through the Rhône and Saône valleys. King Gundobad issued law codes for Burgundians and Romans. Clovis's sons conquered the kingdom in 534, but the name Burgundy endured.",
 conflicts="Destruction of the Worms kingdom by Hunnic auxiliaries (436–437). Wars with the Franks from 523; the kingdom fell in 534 after defeat at Autun (532).",
 leaders="Gundahar (Gunther), killed in 436; Gundobad (c. 473–516), who issued the Lex Gundobada; Sigismund (516–524), who founded the monastery of Saint-Maurice; Godomar (524–534).",
 religion="Mostly Arian, though some royals were Catholic. Sigismund converted to Catholicism and founded the abbey of Agaune (Saint-Maurice, 515), with perpetual chanting by relays of monks.",
 culture="Burgundians lived among Gallo-Roman landowners, receiving shares of estates. Separate laws applied to Burgundians and Romans until they merged. Bishop Avitus of Vienne advised the kings.",
 literature="The Lex Gundobada and the Lex Romana Burgundionum are the main written sources. The later Nibelungenlied and Norse Völsunga saga preserve legends of Gunther's fall.",
 art="Burgundian belt plaques of bronze and bone show Daniel among the lions and other Christian motifs mixed with Germanic interlace.",
 ideas="Gundobad's law tried to treat Burgundians and Romans equally in compensation for injuries, an early attempt at shared justice under one king.",
 science="Bishops and Roman landowners kept Roman land surveying and estate management in the Rhône valley.",
 story=[{"title":"Gunther's Hoard","text":"The real King Gundahar died with his people in 437. In later legend he became Gunther of Worms, who sank the Nibelung treasure in the Rhine and died rather than tell where it lay."}],
 images=[("Abbaye de Saint-Maurice.jpg","Abbey of Saint-Maurice d'Agaune, founded by King Sigismund in 515","culture"),
         ("Nibelungenlied manuscript-k.jpg","Nibelungenlied manuscript, the epic that remembers the Burgundian fall","art")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Burgundians'; I. Wood, The Merovingian Kingdoms 450–751 (1994).")

add(id="alamanni", name="Alamanni", type="tribal confederation", yearsActive="c. 213–496 (independent)",
 summary="A confederation of Germanic tribes on the upper Rhine and Danube, whose name means 'all men'. They settled Alsace and the Swiss plateau and were conquered by Clovis around 496–506.",
 description="The Alamanni appear in Roman sources in 213 as raiders across the Roman frontier. In the fifth century they spread into Alsace, the Palatinate and northern Switzerland. Clovis defeated them, traditionally at Tolbiac around 496, and the rest came under Frankish rule, governed by dukes. Their dialect is the ancestor of Swiss German and Swabian, and the French and Spanish words for Germany (Allemagne, Alemania) come from their name.",
 conflicts="Roman campaigns against them included Julian's victory at Strasbourg (357). Defeat by Clovis (c. 496) and later absorption into the Frankish kingdom; a final revolt was crushed at Cannstatt (746).",
 leaders="Chnodomar led them at Strasbourg (357). Later rulers were Frankish-appointed dukes such as Gotfrid (d. 709).",
 religion="Pagan into the sixth century; converted gradually under Frankish rule and Irish missionaries such as Columbanus and Gallus, whose hermitage became the abbey of St Gall.",
 culture="Farming villages and hill-forts with chieftains. Row-grave cemeteries show warrior burials with swords and gold-foil crosses. The Pactus and Lex Alamannorum record their law.",
 literature="The Lex Alamannorum (early 8th century) records their customary law in Latin.",
 art="Gold-foil crosses sewn onto shrouds and inlaid brooches from row-grave cemeteries.",
 ideas="Their law codes show a society of free farmers, nobles and dependants under ducal and royal authority.",
 science="The St Gall abbey later grew from Gallus's cell into one of the great libraries of Europe.",
 story=[{"title":"Clovis's Prayer","text":"Gregory of Tours says that when the battle against the Alamanni went badly, the pagan Clovis prayed to his wife Clotilde's Christian God, promising to be baptized if he won. The Alamanni broke, and Clovis kept his word."}],
 images=[("Stiftsbibliothek St. Gallen.jpg","Abbey library of St Gall, grown from the cell of the missionary Gallus","culture"),
         ("Goldblattkreuz Lauchheim.jpg","Alamannic gold-foil cross from Lauchheim","art")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Alemanni'; J. F. Drinkwater, The Alamanni and Rome 213–496 (2007).")

add(id="vandals", name="Vandal Kingdom", type="kingdom", yearsActive="429–534",
 summary="An East Germanic people who crossed the Rhine in 406, moved through Gaul and Spain, and in 439 took Carthage, ruling North Africa until Justinian's general Belisarius destroyed the kingdom in 533–534.",
 description="Led by Gaiseric, about 80,000 Vandals and Alans crossed from Spain to Africa in 429. They took Hippo in 431 and Carthage in 439, cutting Rome off from its richest grain province. With a fleet they seized Sardinia, Corsica and the Balearics and sacked Rome in 455. Gaiseric's successors kept the kingdom until Belisarius won at Ad Decimum and Tricamarum (533) and King Gelimer surrendered.",
 conflicts="Sack of Rome (455), carrying off the empress and treasures. Destruction of Roman fleets in 461 and 468. Wars with Moorish kingdoms in the interior. Fall to Belisarius in 533–534.",
 leaders="Gaiseric (428–477); Huneric (477–484), who persecuted Catholics; Thrasamund (496–523); Gelimer (530–534), the last king.",
 religion="Arian Christians who exiled and persecuted many Catholic bishops in Africa. Augustine died at Hippo (430) during their siege.",
 culture="A small Vandal elite enjoyed Roman villas, baths and mosaics in Carthage. Latin poetry flourished at court. Their reputation for destruction gave us the word 'vandalism' only in 1794.",
 literature="Victor of Vita's History of the Vandal Persecution is the main hostile source. Poets such as Luxorius and Dracontius wrote Latin verse at Carthage.",
 art="Floor mosaics from Carthage show Vandal-era horsemen in Roman villa settings.",
 ideas="The kingdom's sea power showed that a Germanic kingdom could control the western Mediterranean, cutting the old empire in two.",
 science="Roman agriculture, irrigation and olive production continued in Africa under Vandal rule.",
 story=[{"title":"Gelimer's Lyre","text":"Besieged on a mountain in 533, the defeated king Gelimer is said to have asked Belisarius's envoy for a loaf of bread, a sponge and a lyre: bread because he had not eaten any in months, a sponge for his tears, and a lyre to sing of his misfortune."}],
 images=[("Vandal cavalryman mosaic Carthage.jpg","Mosaic of a Vandal horseman from Carthage (c. 500)","art"),
         ("Gelimer coin.jpg","Coin of Gelimer, last Vandal king","culture")],
 sources="Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Vandal'; A. Merrills and R. Miles, The Vandals (2010).")
